  "learningBlocks": [
    {
      "id": "l5-b1",
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "The verb ending tells you who acts",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Present active verbs now add person and number to the information you can read from endings. The central contrast is Credo = I believe versus Laudamus/Benedicimus/Adoramus/Glorificamus = we praise/bless/adore/glorify.",
        "fr": "Les verbes actifs au présent ajoutent maintenant la personne et le nombre aux informations données par les terminaisons. Le contraste central est Credo = je crois et Laudamus/Benedicimus/Adoramus/Glorificamus = nous louons/bénissons/adorons/glorifions."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "La terminaison verbale indique qui agit"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l5-b2",
      "type": "verb-system",
      "title": "Read person and number",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Use the ending before translating mechanically: -o often signals first-person singular; -mus signals first-person plural. Other present endings become familiar through the assigned verbs and later lessons.",
        "fr": "Lisez d'abord la terminaison : -o signale souvent la première personne du singulier; -mus la première personne du pluriel. Les autres terminaisons du présent se fixeront avec les verbes du cours."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Lire la personne et le nombre"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l5-b3",
      "type": "authentic-example",
      "title": "Four verbs, one ending pattern",
      "sourceId": "mr1962-gloria-verbs-l5",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "The Gloria gives four consecutive -mus forms. That repetition is pedagogically ideal: the vocabulary changes, but the grammatical person and number stay constant.",
        "fr": "Le Gloria donne quatre formes successives en -mus. La répétition est idéale : le vocabulaire change, mais la personne et le nombre restent identiques."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "mr1962-gloria-verbs-l5",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Quatre verbes, un même schéma de terminaison"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l5-b4",
      "type": "authentic-example",
      "title": "Credo",
      "sourceId": "mr1962-credo-opening-l5",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Credo is first-person singular: 'I believe'. Compare that single speaker with the congregation's plural Laudamus, Benedicimus, Adoramus, Glorificamus.",
        "fr": "Credo est à la première personne du singulier : « je crois ». Comparez ce sujet singulier au pluriel de Laudamus, Benedicimus, Adoramus, Glorificamus."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "mr1962-credo-opening-l5",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Credo"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l5-b5",
      "type": "controlled-practice",
      "title": "Apply the same system to new verbs",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "audio, servio, venio, habeo, amo, video, voco, mitto, maneo, aperio, pono, sto and timeo are learned as verb families. Controlled exercises use their present forms so the learner sees person/number rather than memorizing disconnected translations.",
        "fr": "audio, servio, venio, habeo, amo, video, voco, mitto, maneo, aperio, pono, sto et timeo sont appris comme familles verbales. Les exercices emploient leurs formes du présent pour faire lire la personne et le nombre."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Appliquer le même système à de nouveaux verbes"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l5-b6",
      "type": "stage-checkpoint",
      "title": "Stage 1 reading checkpoint",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "The checkpoint mixes everything earned in Lessons 1–5: sound/stress, the permanent case map, first/second declensions, sum, adjective agreement, and present active person/number. Later grammar remains glossed rather than silently tested.",
        "fr": "Le contrôle de fin d'étape mélange tout ce qui a été acquis dans les leçons 1 à 5 : prononciation, carte des cas, première/deuxième déclinaisons, sum, accord de l'adjectif et personne/nombre du présent actif. La grammaire ultérieure reste explicitement fournie."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Jalon de lecture de l’étape 1"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l5-b7",
      "type": "retrieval",
      "title": "From phrase to sentence",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Stage 1 ends when the learner can move through a short authentic sentence and identify several grammatical anchors without translating every word. That is the first phrase → sentence transition.",
        "fr": "L'étape 1 est achevée lorsque l'apprenant peut parcourir une courte phrase authentique et y repérer plusieurs points d'appui grammaticaux sans traduire chaque mot. C'est le premier passage de la formule à la phrase."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Du groupe de mots à la phrase"
        }
      }
    }
  ],
